    metaDesc:'North Macedonia\u2019s international schooling is entirely in Skopje while Tetovo has an English-medium university, Ohrid hosts Europe every summer, and a vast diaspora is coming home mid-curriculum. The guide for everyone outside the capital.',
    intro:'North Macedonia is a small country that has been internationally connected for a long time — through its industrial zones, through Lake Ohrid\u2019s summer, through an English-medium university in Tetovo, and above all through a diaspora that is enormous relative to its population and increasingly coming home. Almost none of that is visible in its school map, which places every international school in Skopje. This article is about the families outside the capital and the ones arriving back into a system they have never attended — and about what a live online school can honestly do for them in a country whose law is stricter than any of its neighbours\u2019.',
    sections:[
      {h:'The returning-diaspora problem, at scale',p:'Germany, Switzerland, Italy, Australia, the United States — North Macedonian families are established in all of them, and the return flow is real, driven by business, family, and property as much as sentiment. The child is frequently ten, thirteen, or fifteen rather than five, and arrives mid-curriculum into a system that does not run that curriculum, sometimes with a home language that is fluent at the dinner table and unfamiliar in a physics lesson. The standard options are to restart inside the national system, losing momentum at precisely the wrong age, or to relocate to Skopje for a campus place. A live international school offers a third that works within the law here: the local school takes the enrolment and every legal obligation, while the Cambridge or IB curriculum the child was already in continues alongside it, with the same examination board and no lost year.'},
      {h:'Tetovo: an English-medium university with no English-medium school beneath it',p:'South East European University teaches in English, Albanian, and Macedonian, and draws students and academics from across the region into the Polog valley. It is a genuine English-medium destination — and beneath it sits a school system with no English-medium route at all. Families in Tetovo and Gostivar who are aiming their children at SEEU\u2019s English-taught programmes, or at universities in Switzerland, Germany, or Italy where much of the extended family already lives, face a preparation gap the valley cannot fill locally. A supplementary Cambridge track fills it precisely, and it is the same qualification all three destinations read.'},
      {h:'The rest of the map',p:'Bitola is the second city, a university town beside a major energy complex with the Greek border half an hour south — and three hours from Skopje. Ohrid runs a UNESCO tourism economy that fills with Europe every summer and empties of international schooling every September. Kumanovo sits on the Corridor X crossroads with one of the country\u2019s strongest diaspora networks. Štip supplies European fashion brands from a textile industry that brought foreign investment into an eastern industrial zone. Each has an internationally connected workforce; none has an international school. For all of them the arithmetic is the same: identical live teaching delivered home, with Skopje needed only for examination sittings a few times a year.'},
      {h:'What we can and cannot do here — stated plainly',p:'North Macedonian law is stricter than any of its neighbours\u2019, and it shapes our offer completely. Parental-choice homeschooling is not established. Compulsory education extends through the secondary level, so there is no post-fifteen phase in which the obligation falls away — the opening that our Croatian, Albanian, Greek, Serbian, and Bulgarian pages describe genuinely does not exist here. Everything we do is therefore supplementary: the school keeps the enrolment and the routine, we teach Cambridge or IB subjects live in the after-school slot toward external examinations. I would rather set that out in an article than have a family discover it after enrolling somewhere that was less careful.'},
      {h:'And where the local school is the right answer',p:'A child settled in a good local school, learning in Macedonian or Albanian, heading toward a national matura and a domestic or regional university does not need us, and the country\u2019s selective gymnasiums do genuinely good work. Our value here is narrow and real: continuity for a diaspora child arriving mid-curriculum, an English-medium academic track where none exists within hours, the subjects a small school cannot staff for three pupils, and a qualification the family\u2019s likely destinations read without translation. Where none of that applies, the local school will serve a child well and I would rather say so.'},
    ],
    faqs:[
      {q:'Are there international schools outside Skopje?',a:'Essentially none. Tetovo, Bitola, Ohrid, Kumanovo, and Štip have no international provision, and Skopje is between one and three hours away depending on where you are.'},
      {q:'We are moving back from Germany or Switzerland mid-curriculum — what are our options?',a:'Enrol locally, which carries every legal obligation, and keep the international pathway running alongside it. The curriculum, teachers, and examination board continue across the move rather than restarting inside a new system.'},
      {q:'Can we take our child out of school for full-time online study?',a:'No — parental-choice homeschooling is not established here, and compulsory education extends through the secondary level. Our arrangement is supplementary alongside your school enrolment, at every age.'},
      {q:'How do class times work?',a:'North Macedonia is one to two hours ahead of our Nairobi teaching base, so live classes land inside the school day and afternoon, with every session recorded.'},
    ],
    conclusion:'A country this internationally connected keeps every international school in one city — and a law that leaves us one honest configuration. Supplementary, live, delivered identically to Tetovo, Ohrid, or Štip, is what we can offer, and it is enough for the families who need it. Book an assessment for your child, or start with our Tetovo and Ohrid pages.',
